f75389b86f3d8e9c,https://openalex.org/W4297631950,https://openalex.org/W4200634402,A Review of Sparse Expert Models in Deep Learning,GLaM: Efficient Scaling of Language Models with Mixture-of-Experts,6,INTRODUCTION,name,unknown,"The state-of-the-art sparse models for few-shot inference (GLaM (Du et al., 2021)) and for fine-tuning (ST-MoE (Zoph et al., 2022)) use only up to 64 larger experts – a better balance of computation and parameters.","ed to thousands of relatively small experts per layer, which has produced excellent pre-training and translation quality (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Fedus et al., 2021). However, the quality of sparse models is disproportionately reduced under domain shift (Artetxe et al., 2021) or when fine-tuning on different task distributions (Fedus et al., 2021). The state-of-the-art sparse models for few-shot inference (GLaM (Du et al., 2021)) and for fine-tuning (ST-MoE (Zoph et al., 2022)) use only up to 64 larger experts – a better balance of computation and parameters. As a result of the increased expert dimensions, these models require specific systemlevel sharding strategies over accelerators to run efficiently (Du et al., 2021; Rajbhandari et al., 2022). Next, we recap the current conventions around the frequency of expert layers. Usually,",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.01667#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,22229
