da013cf7fb92441e,https://openalex.org/W4391420732,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,LLaVA-MoLE: Sparse Mixture of LoRA Experts for Mitigating Data Conflicts in Instruction Finetuning MLLMs,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,4,Method,numeric,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"In our case, since the instruction data is relatively small compared to the text corpus used in previous works [12, 20], we decide to raise the expert capacity to the maximum context length of the LLM so that no token is dropped and the experts receive sufficient training.","imizes the router weights. As Llb reduces, the expert assignment becomes closer to uniform. Previous works set an expert capacity that ensures each expert can not process a number of tokens that exceeds the given capacity (the overflowed tokens are dropped), thus strictly limits the computation load of each expert. In our case, since the instruction data is relatively small compared to the text corpus used in previous works [12, 20], we decide to raise the expert capacity to the maximum context length of the LLM so that no token is dropped and the experts receive sufficient training. 4. Experiments In this section, we present the experimental results of our proposed method on various data configurations. 4.1. Model Architecture The basic model architecture follows the design of LLaVA1.5 [23], where a CLIP ViT-L [30] is used as the vision encoder, w",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.16160#page=4,heuristic_unvalidated,17374
147e3eca51fa17af,https://openalex.org/W4391591172,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,CompeteSMoE -- Effective Training of Sparse Mixture of Experts via Competition,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,5,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"However, since activating all experts for every input is expensive for large models, a sparse version (Shazeer et al., 2017) (SMoE) is often more attractive since it offers low computational costs while enjoying improved representation capabilities.","ergence rate for density estimations (Mendes & Jiang, 2012; Norets & Pelenis, 2021; 2022) and the problem of parameter estimation (Ho et al., 2022; Nguyen et al., 2023a; 2024c;a). Investigating MoE to handle complex data structures such as vision or speech is an equally attractive research direction (Eigen et al., 2013). However, since activating all experts for every input is expensive for large models, a sparse version (Shazeer et al., 2017) (SMoE) is often more attractive since it offers low computational costs while enjoying improved representation capabilities. To this end, SMoE has been widely explored to improved the training efficiency of LLMs, with various routing strategies proposed, from (i) letting tokens select the top-k experts (Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2022; Zuo et al., 2022; Chi et al., 2022; Dai et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2023), (ii)",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.02526#page=5,heuristic_unvalidated,22043
aea32de47ab1fa8c,https://openalex.org/W4391591172,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,CompeteSMoE -- Effective Training of Sparse Mixture of Experts via Competition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Background,author_year,background_section,We follow Fedus et al. (2022) and only implement SMoE in the fully connected layers.,", we conduct extensive experiments to demonstrate strong learning capabilities of CompeteSMoE and show its promising scalability to large scale architectures. 2. Background Table 6 in the Appendix summarizes all notations used in this work. We first describe the SMoE training process, which consists of a router R(·, Wr) parameterized by Wr and N experts {g(·, Wei)}N i=1 parameterized by Wei, i ∈ [N], respectively. We follow Fedus et al. (2022) and only implement SMoE in the fully connected layers. Given an input token x and its representation z ∈Rd, SMoE first uses its router to calculate the affinity scores between z and each experts as s = R(h). Then, a sparse gating function, TopK, is applied to select only K experts with the largest affinity scores. Here we define the TopK function as: TopK(vi, K) := ( vi, if vi is in the K largest elements of v −∞, otherwise.",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.02526#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6556
