6f98c55983bca6b6,https://openalex.org/W4399836663,https://openalex.org/W4396815229,DeepSeek-Coder-V2: Breaking the Barrier of Closed-Source Models in Code Intelligence,"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model",14,Model,name,unknown,"On the other hand, DeepSeek-V2 Chat demonstrates slightly better results in benchmarks such as MT-bench (Zheng et al., 2023), AlpacaEval 2.0 (Dubois et al., 2024), and AlignBench (Liu et al., 2023c).","els at. However, DeepSeek-Coder-V2-Lite Instruct falls behind in knowledge-intensive benchmarks like TriviaQA, primarily due to the relatively smaller amount of web data used during pre-training. Moving on to 236B models, DeepSeek-Coder-V2 Instruct exhibits greater strength in reasoning benchmarks, particularly in Arena-Hard, which comprises a substantial proportion of code, math, and reasoning questions. On the other hand, DeepSeek-V2 Chat demonstrates slightly better results in benchmarks such as MT-bench (Zheng et al., 2023), AlpacaEval 2.0 (Dubois et al., 2024), and AlignBench (Liu et al., 2023c). This advantage can be attributed to the general-purpose alignment stage of DeepSeek-V2 Chat. 5. Conclusion In this paper, we introduce DeepSeek-Coder-V2 to further advance the field of code intelligence, which is continually pre-trained from DeepSeek-V2 with 6",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.11931#page=14,heuristic_unvalidated,41946
d1c6a3c7d7c92da3,https://openalex.org/W4399836663,https://openalex.org/W4396815229,DeepSeek-Coder-V2: Breaking the Barrier of Closed-Source Models in Code Intelligence,"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model",14,Model,name,unknown,This advantage can be attributed to the general-purpose alignment stage of DeepSeek-V2 Chat.,"s greater strength in reasoning benchmarks, particularly in Arena-Hard, which comprises a substantial proportion of code, math, and reasoning questions. On the other hand, DeepSeek-V2 Chat demonstrates slightly better results in benchmarks such as MT-bench (Zheng et al., 2023), AlpacaEval 2.0 (Dubois et al., 2024), and AlignBench (Liu et al., 2023c). This advantage can be attributed to the general-purpose alignment stage of DeepSeek-V2 Chat. 5. Conclusion In this paper, we introduce DeepSeek-Coder-V2 to further advance the field of code intelligence, which is continually pre-trained from DeepSeek-V2 with 6 trillion tokens sourced from a highquality and multi-source corpus. Through this continued pre-training, we find that DeepSeek14 Coder-V2 significantly enhances the model’s capabilities in coding and mathematical reasoning while maintaining comparabl",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.11931#page=14,heuristic_unvalidated,42202
613e4c77b7192854,https://openalex.org/W4399836663,https://openalex.org/W4396815229,DeepSeek-Coder-V2: Breaking the Barrier of Closed-Source Models in Code Intelligence,"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model",14,Conclusion,name,unknown,"Conclusion In this paper, we introduce DeepSeek-Coder-V2 to further advance the field of code intelligence, which is continually pre-trained from DeepSeek-V2 with 6 trillion tokens sourced from a highquality and multi-source corpus.","hand, DeepSeek-V2 Chat demonstrates slightly better results in benchmarks such as MT-bench (Zheng et al., 2023), AlpacaEval 2.0 (Dubois et al., 2024), and AlignBench (Liu et al., 2023c). This advantage can be attributed to the general-purpose alignment stage of DeepSeek-V2 Chat. 5. Conclusion In this paper, we introduce DeepSeek-Coder-V2 to further advance the field of code intelligence, which is continually pre-trained from DeepSeek-V2 with 6 trillion tokens sourced from a highquality and multi-source corpus. Through this continued pre-training, we find that DeepSeek14 Coder-V2 significantly enhances the model’s capabilities in coding and mathematical reasoning while maintaining comparable general language performance to DeepSeek-V2. Compared to DeepSeek-Coder, DeepSeek-Coder-V2 supports a significantly larger number of programming languages, increasin",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.11931#page=14,heuristic_unvalidated,42369
088d61dd39e46faf,https://openalex.org/W4399836663,https://openalex.org/W4396815229,DeepSeek-Coder-V2: Breaking the Barrier of Closed-Source Models in Code Intelligence,"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model",15,Conclusion,name,unknown,Coder-V2 significantly enhances the model’s capabilities in coding and mathematical reasoning while maintaining comparable general language performance to DeepSeek-V2.,", we introduce DeepSeek-Coder-V2 to further advance the field of code intelligence, which is continually pre-trained from DeepSeek-V2 with 6 trillion tokens sourced from a highquality and multi-source corpus. Through this continued pre-training, we find that DeepSeek14 Coder-V2 significantly enhances the model’s capabilities in coding and mathematical reasoning while maintaining comparable general language performance to DeepSeek-V2. Compared to DeepSeek-Coder, DeepSeek-Coder-V2 supports a significantly larger number of programming languages, increasing from 86 to 338, and extends the maximum context length from 16K to 128K tokens. Experimental results demonstrate that DeepSeek-Coder-V2 achieves performance comparable to state-of-the-art closed-source models such as GPT-4 Turbo, Claude 3 Opus, and Gemini 1.5 Pro in code and math-specific tasks. Although",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.11931#page=15,heuristic_unvalidated,42677
f1f151ba97ec44cd,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,1,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>.","61, 33]. As these models grow in accordance with scaling laws [21], the norm has shifted towards architectures with billions to trillions of parameters. However, the larger scale brings considerable deployment challenges due to increased demands on memory, processing power, and energy consumption [65, 53]. In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>. Preprint. arXiv:2407.00945v1 [cs.LG] 1 Jul 2024 (a) A SMoE block before pruning. (b) Parameter space designed for expert pruning and merging. Figure 1: (a) the original SMoE block",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2826
6a6f4e0c5a3a46ce,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,3,Related work,numeric,background_section,"[45] introduced the sparse MoE layer, which consists of multiple experts, each being a simple feed-forward network (FFN), and a trainable router network that selects a sparse combination of the experts to process each input.","In addition, to inherit knowledge from the unpruned model, existing methods either select a subset of weights based on predefined importance criteria [16, 60, 38], or rely on distillation techniques [39, 62, 1]. In contrast, EEP introduces a novel approach as a third paradigm, employing weight merging [56] to transfer knowledge during the model compression process. 2 Related work Sparse Mixture-of-Experts LLMs. Shazeer et al. [45] introduced the sparse MoE layer, which consists of multiple experts, each being a simple feed-forward network (FFN), and a trainable router network that selects a sparse combination of the experts to process each input. Such SMoE models can significantly increase model capacity while maintaining computational efficiency. However, this utility is ideally achieved when the router accurately and evenly assigns experts to each to",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,9390
652120dd89d00382,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W3040573126,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding,1,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>.","61, 33]. As these models grow in accordance with scaling laws [21], the norm has shifted towards architectures with billions to trillions of parameters. However, the larger scale brings considerable deployment challenges due to increased demands on memory, processing power, and energy consumption [65, 53]. In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>. Preprint. arXiv:2407.00945v1 [cs.LG] 1 Jul 2024 (a) A SMoE block before pruning. (b) Parameter space designed for expert pruning and merging. Figure 1: (a) the original SMoE block",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2826
6d5b00086f8ab234,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,1,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>.","61, 33]. As these models grow in accordance with scaling laws [21], the norm has shifted towards architectures with billions to trillions of parameters. However, the larger scale brings considerable deployment challenges due to increased demands on memory, processing power, and energy consumption [65, 53]. In response to these challenges, there is a notable trend towards adopting sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures [45, 14, 27, 19], as seen in models such as Mixtral 8 × 7B and 8 × 22B [20], Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B [4], Qwen 2-57B-A14B [40], DBRX [50], Correspondence to Yu Wang <yu-wang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn>, Zinan Lin <zinanlin@microsoft.com>, Xuefei Ning <foxdoraame@gmail.com>. Preprint. arXiv:2407.00945v1 [cs.LG] 1 Jul 2024 (a) A SMoE block before pruning. (b) Parameter space designed for expert pruning and merging. Figure 1: (a) the original SMoE block",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2826
f8d00e5475d2895d,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,3,Related work,numeric,background_section,"Many works address these challenges [14, 28, 12, 64].","ach being a simple feed-forward network (FFN), and a trainable router network that selects a sparse combination of the experts to process each input. Such SMoE models can significantly increase model capacity while maintaining computational efficiency. However, this utility is ideally achieved when the router accurately and evenly assigns experts to each token during training and inference. Many works address these challenges [14, 28, 12, 64]. Recently, many SOTA LLMs adopt the SMoE structure to achieve high performance and computational efficiency simultaneously [20, 4, 50, 57]. Additionally, Zhang et al. [63] propose transforming non-MoE models into SMoE models to accelerate inference, and Komatsuzaki et al. [25] upcycle pretrained models by reusing the parameters to initialize SMoE models, where all experts are replicates of the original FFNs, and then fine-tun",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,9895
6c58fade9007bb8b,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,10,Method,numeric,unknown,"The issue of imbalanced activation has been identified in several works [14, 9].","n the remaining parameters are not retrained. Our hypothesis is that the router network operates differently after expert pruning, leading to this improvement. Typically, the router network is implemented as a smaller network, such as a one-layer perceptron. This makes it challenging to accurately partition the high-dimensional hidden space among experts. The issue of imbalanced activation has been identified in several works [14, 9]. If the router network does not function optimally before pruning, there may be potential for improvement by enabling the router to focus on a smaller subset of experts. Although it is difficult to directly evaluate the router network’s performance, we have observed that its behavior changes significantly after pruning. This change occurs because the pruning process eliminates some experts, and the routing weights for the rem",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=10,heuristic_unvalidated,36100
f34622d5a889ecf3,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4224211001,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,On the Representation Collapse of Sparse Mixture of Experts,10,Method,numeric,unknown,"The issue of imbalanced activation has been identified in several works [14, 9].","n the remaining parameters are not retrained. Our hypothesis is that the router network operates differently after expert pruning, leading to this improvement. Typically, the router network is implemented as a smaller network, such as a one-layer perceptron. This makes it challenging to accurately partition the high-dimensional hidden space among experts. The issue of imbalanced activation has been identified in several works [14, 9]. If the router network does not function optimally before pruning, there may be potential for improvement by enabling the router to focus on a smaller subset of experts. Although it is difficult to directly evaluate the router network’s performance, we have observed that its behavior changes significantly after pruning. This change occurs because the pruning process eliminates some experts, and the routing weights for the rem",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=10,heuristic_unvalidated,36100
346a79fcf49499cb,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4224299101,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,StableMoE: Stable Routing Strategy for Mixture of Experts,3,Related work,numeric,background_section,"Many works address these challenges [14, 28, 12, 64].","ach being a simple feed-forward network (FFN), and a trainable router network that selects a sparse combination of the experts to process each input. Such SMoE models can significantly increase model capacity while maintaining computational efficiency. However, this utility is ideally achieved when the router accurately and evenly assigns experts to each token during training and inference. Many works address these challenges [14, 28, 12, 64]. Recently, many SOTA LLMs adopt the SMoE structure to achieve high performance and computational efficiency simultaneously [20, 4, 50, 57]. Additionally, Zhang et al. [63] propose transforming non-MoE models into SMoE models to accelerate inference, and Komatsuzaki et al. [25] upcycle pretrained models by reusing the parameters to initialize SMoE models, where all experts are replicates of the original FFNs, and then fine-tun",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,9895
4c000d9e0b5685f8,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4311252752,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Sparse Upcycling: Training Mixture-of-Experts from Dense Checkpoints,3,Related work,numeric,background_section,"[25] upcycle pretrained models by reusing the parameters to initialize SMoE models, where all experts are replicates of the original FFNs, and then fine-tune the SMoE models.","eved when the router accurately and evenly assigns experts to each token during training and inference. Many works address these challenges [14, 28, 12, 64]. Recently, many SOTA LLMs adopt the SMoE structure to achieve high performance and computational efficiency simultaneously [20, 4, 50, 57]. Additionally, Zhang et al. [63] propose transforming non-MoE models into SMoE models to accelerate inference, and Komatsuzaki et al. [25] upcycle pretrained models by reusing the parameters to initialize SMoE models, where all experts are replicates of the original FFNs, and then fine-tune the SMoE models. Pruning for LLMs. Pruning techniques have emerged as a crucial strategy for optimizing LLMs by reducing model size and computational costs while maintaining performance. Unstructured pruning [6, 15, 47, 48] entails the removal of individual weights according",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,10185
0bf85e3a1700c9be,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"Recent expert pruning methods achieve 25%-50% sparsity and accelerate inference, but struggle to maintain performance [34] or need fine-tuning, requiring substantial GPU memory and resources [8, 37].","ng performance [6, 15, 47], or structured, removing entire channels or layers to provide computational efficiency and reduced latency [35, 49, 58, 18, 54, 26]. One particularly efficient way is expert pruning in SMoE LLMs, a type of structured pruning with coarse granularity, which enhances overall efficiency. Recent expert pruning methods achieve 25%-50% sparsity and accelerate inference, but struggle to maintain performance [34] or need fine-tuning, requiring substantial GPU memory and resources [8, 37]. Thus, there is a pressing need for efficient pruning methods that operate within the constraints of inference resources for SMoE LLMs. In this work, we propose a gradient-free evolutionary strategy that achieves high sparsity while maintaining performance given a small train set on the downstream tasks. Our method is divided into two phases: expert p",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,5338
1f080ff1125a479d,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,2,Introduction,numeric,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"Our approach enables more aggressive pruning of experts compared to current methods [34, 37].","experts. To these ends, WRM and WEM are set to one-hot rows in the first phase and to real numbers in the second phase. Since our method is gradient-free, it can be conducted on devices capable of inference. Our contributions can be summarized as follows: • Pruning the total number of experts: smaller memory consumption and better performance. Our approach enables more aggressive pruning of experts compared to current methods [34, 37]. In experiments on Mixtral 8×7B-Instruct, we reduce the number of experts in each SMoE block from 8 to 2, a 72% reduction in parameters, while maintaining comparable performance across various downstream tasks. Surprisingly, we observe that fewer experts can lead to better performance. For instance, on the SQuAD dataset, pruning 4 out of 8 experts result in a performance increase from 53.4% to 75.4% without updating the remai",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6775
3d649529ef955159,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,3,Related work,numeric,background_section,"Additionally, expert pruning for SMoE models selectively prunes the expert networks [34, 37, 8, 24].","s, such as neurons or attention heads, facilitating more straightforward implementation on standard hardware. Within structured pruning, specific focus areas include attention mechanisms, where redundant heads are pruned to streamline the self-attention layers, and FFNs where unnecessary neurons are removed to enhance computational efficiency. Additionally, expert pruning for SMoE models selectively prunes the expert networks [34, 37, 8, 24]. Evolutionary Strategy for Optimization. Evolutionary Strategies (ES) have been increasingly recognized for their robustness and flexibility in various optimization tasks, particularly where gradientbased methods fall short [55]. Notably, ES is highly effective for optimizing non-differentiable objective functions, offering a powerful alternative in scenarios where gradients are unavailable or unreliable [43, 22, 32, 52,",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,11249
9dda1f72bc6b660f,https://openalex.org/W4400341518,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,Efficient Expert Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts Language Models: Enhancing Performance and Reducing Inference Costs,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,6,Method,numeric,unknown,"4.3 Evolutionary search for the router mapping and expert merging matrices The search space of the router mapping and expert merging matrices is large and complex, making it difficult to design heuristics for determining a solution, as is done in other expert pruning studies [37, 8, 34].",", the elements of WRM and WEM transition from discrete 0/1 values to continuous values. This allows the matrices to perform more nuanced transformations. 4.3 Evolutionary search for the router mapping and expert merging matrices The search space of the router mapping and expert merging matrices is large and complex, making it difficult to design heuristics for determining a solution, as is done in other expert pruning studies [37, 8, 34]. Therefore, an efficient optimization strategy is necessary. Given the substantial size of SMoE LLMs, computing gradients for optimization is computationally prohibitive for most users. As a solution, we employ a gradient-free evolutionary strategy, similar to approaches found in previous works [30, 32]. Our algorithm is detailed in Alg. 1. Initially, we populate the search space using random initialization. During the evolut",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.00945#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,19694
