992aaa40c763253e,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4311252752,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,Sparse Upcycling: Training Mixture-of-Experts from Dense Checkpoints,2,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"Conversely, Komatsuzaki et al. (2022) directly copy the FFNs to form experts.","during an internship at Shanghai AI Laboratory. Technical Report. arXiv:2406.16554v1 [cs.CL] 24 Jun 2024 To build strong LLaMA-MoE models, we identify two important challenges. First, how to effectively construct experts from the Feed-Forward Networks (FFNs) in the existing LLMs. There are works exploring splitting FFN parameters to construct experts on T5 (Zhang et al., 2021) or BERT (Zuo et al., 2022) models. Conversely, Komatsuzaki et al. (2022) directly copy the FFNs to form experts. However, there is no existing work exploring it for decoder-only models. Notably, the Swish-based FFN (Ramachandran et al., 2017; Shazeer, 2020) does not bring natural sparsity as ReLU (Nair and Hinton, 2010) in T5 and BERT. Second, overcoming the performance decrease entailed by changing the network structure from dense to sparse remains challenging. Due to the reduction in the amount",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,3502
a62e8b2a0ad4291c,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4311252752,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,Sparse Upcycling: Training Mixture-of-Experts from Dense Checkpoints,3,Related Work,name,background_section,"Sparse upcycling (Komatsuzaki et al., 2022) explore upgrading an existing dense model into a larger, sparsely activated MoE.","E Expert 1 Expert 2 Expert 3 token vector Rescaling Gate Figure 1: The main framework of building LLaMA-MoE models. (a) The original FFNs in the LLaMA are split into different experts. (b) In the transformed LLaMA-MoE, the hidden states are processed by partially chosen experts instead of all experts. We continue to train the LLaMA-MoE to improve the performance. keeping the activation parameters as the original dense models. Sparse upcycling (Komatsuzaki et al., 2022) explore upgrading an existing dense model into a larger, sparsely activated MoE. In particular, the experts in the new MoE layer are identical copies of the original MLP layer that is replaced. In this paper, our work follows the first research line and decomposes the original FFNs into multiple small experts. Different from MoEBERT and MoEfication, our work focuses on a SwiGLU-based decoder-style m",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,8439
9db36529de01ae4c,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4378473852,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,Mixture-of-Experts Meets Instruction Tuning:A Winning Combination for Large Language Models,5,Model,author_year,unknown,"Wikipedia) have been proven to be less effective on downstream tasks (Shen et al., 2023b).","3a). To improve the training efficiency, we explore different data sampling strategies and data quality filtering methods as follows. Data Sampling Weights. The data sampling weights are crucial to obtain a global optimum (Xie et al., 2023). LLaMA-v1 utilizes a set of static empirical sampling weights (Touvron et al., 2023a), while some of the domains (e.g. Wikipedia) have been proven to be less effective on downstream tasks (Shen et al., 2023b). This indicates it may be not appropriate to assign large weights when sampling these domains. Xie et al. (2023) employ additional models to obtain better static sampling weights. Although it is faster to get convergence, it brings additional training compute. Xia et al. (2023) introduce a dynamic weight sampling strategy in the training phase, which boosts performance on downstream tasks. To obtain better performances, we i",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=5,heuristic_unvalidated,15104
4395928462f00813,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4378473852,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,Mixture-of-Experts Meets Instruction Tuning:A Winning Combination for Large Language Models,10,Model,author_year,unknown,"5.10 Instruction Tuning MoE models have shown great prowess in instruction tuning with excellent task scaling properties (Shen et al., 2023a; Chen et al., 2024; Zhu et al., 2024).","tinual pre-training to further improve downstream performances. For example, the model may consume more tokens from arXiv to improve GSM-8K results. However, expert selections on ARC-c and GSM-8K have greater distances with current pre-training data, which may involve new domains to deal with such tasks. 5.10 Instruction Tuning MoE models have shown great prowess in instruction tuning with excellent task scaling properties (Shen et al., 2023a; Chen et al., 2024; Zhu et al., 2024). To evaluate the instructed MoE models’ performances, we fine-tune LLaMA-MoE with 6k curated ShareGPT instruction data (Liu et al., 2023) for 2 epochs. As shown in Table 3, the instructed LLaMA-MoE-3.5B (4/16) outperforms the dense model on ARC-C (48.29 vs. 43.69) and HellaSwag (75.10 vs. 71.70) tasks. The overall performance on Open LLM Leaderboard 2 tasks surpasses the dense model (48.9",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=10,heuristic_unvalidated,32394
17a61198432f9001,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4399795085,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,$\texttt{MoE-RBench}$: Towards Building Reliable Language Models with Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,10,Model,author_year,unknown,"5.10 Instruction Tuning MoE models have shown great prowess in instruction tuning with excellent task scaling properties (Shen et al., 2023a; Chen et al., 2024; Zhu et al., 2024).","to further improve downstream performances. For example, the model may consume more tokens from arXiv to improve GSM-8K results. However, expert selections on ARC-c and GSM-8K have greater distances with current pre-training data, which may involve new domains to deal with such tasks. 5.10 Instruction Tuning MoE models have shown great prowess in instruction tuning with excellent task scaling properties (Shen et al., 2023a; Chen et al., 2024; Zhu et al., 2024). To evaluate the instructed MoE models’ performances, we fine-tune LLaMA-MoE with 6k curated ShareGPT instruction data (Liu et al., 2023) for 2 epochs. As shown in Table 3, the instructed LLaMA-MoE-3.5B (4/16) outperforms the dense model on ARC-C (48.29 vs. 43.69) and HellaSwag (75.10 vs. 71.70) tasks. The overall performance on Open LLM Leaderboard 2 tasks surpasses the dense model (48.95 vs. 47.41). Besid",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=10,heuristic_unvalidated,32414
8f1139f151133aaf,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W4402671950,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,DeepSeekMoE: Towards Ultimate Expert Specialization in Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,1,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"However, training MoEs from scratch (Fedus et al., 2022b; Zoph et al., 2022; Xue et al., 2024; Dai et al., 2024) leads to a significant overall budget.","d by the trade-off between scaling the model size and reducing the inference cost, sparse Mixture-ofExperts (MoE) is further proposed to activate a part of the model parameters (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020). Inspired by this, we focus on sparsely activated MoE models that decouple model size from computation costs. However, training MoEs from scratch (Fedus et al., 2022b; Zoph et al., 2022; Xue et al., 2024; Dai et al., 2024) leads to a significant overall budget. In this work, we reduce the training costs by investigating building MoE models from existing dense LLMs. Moreover, starting from the dense model provides flexible structure design choices for MoE. In other words, we can place the MoE block in any transformer layers. In this paper, we dedicate to building a full MoE model from LLaMA (Touvron et al., 2023b), where each layer contains an",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2600
0b38d106626151ff,https://openalex.org/W4404782031,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,LLaMA-MoE: Building Mixture-of-Experts from LLaMA with Continual Pre-Training,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,2,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"For optimizing the training, BASE layer (Lewis et al., 2021), HASH layer (Roller et al., 2021), and Expert Choice (Zhou et al., 2022) study how to build MoE models to fully utilize the model capacity.","0 languages. Switch Transformers (Fedus et al., 2022b) further scales the language model’s size to the trillion-level parameter with a simple and effective MoE layer design. Naively trained MoE models is prone to load imbalance, e.g., only a few experts are frequently used while the others are scarcely activated. For optimizing the training, BASE layer (Lewis et al., 2021), HASH layer (Roller et al., 2021), and Expert Choice (Zhou et al., 2022) study how to build MoE models to fully utilize the model capacity. Recently, for model architecture, Xue et al. (2024) explore training a decoder-only MoE with a modified UL2 training objective. Mixtral is another decoder-style MoE model that selects two out of eight experts with token-choice routing (AI, 2023b). Expert Construction. There are two lines of works constructing MoE from dense checkpoints. The first category spl",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2406.16554#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6616
f86cd960bcf55bb4,https://openalex.org/W4404782838,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Parameter-Efficient Sparsity Crafting from Dense to Mixture-of-Experts for Instruction Tuning on General Tasks,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,2,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"(2023) presented a method for upcycling dense models into sparse activated MoE models, which boast greater capacity (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Fedus et al., 2022; Puigcerver et al., 2023).","s capacity can also improve instruction tuning effectiveness for general tasks (Kaplan et al., 2020). Nonetheless, 1 arXiv:2401.02731v4 [cs.AI] 24 Sep 2024 most LLMs are pre-trained dense models designed based on transformer architecture, which limits scalability during instruction tuning. Komatsuzaki et al. (2023) presented a method for upcycling dense models into sparse activated MoE models, which boast greater capacity (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Fedus et al., 2022; Puigcerver et al., 2023). Notably, Shen et al. (2023) suggested that MoE models respond more effectively to instruction tuning compared to dense models. Consequently, converting dense models into MoE models during instruction tuning has the potential to achieve great performance on general tasks. This conversion involves initializing each expert in the MoE models as a copy of",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.02731#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,3858
56a4a29de11a9d00,https://openalex.org/W4404782838,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Parameter-Efficient Sparsity Crafting from Dense to Mixture-of-Experts for Instruction Tuning on General Tasks,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,7,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"In contrast, sparse models, employing the MoE architecture (Shazeer et al., 2017), activate only a subset of the total available parameters for each input token.","variation in expert selection across different datasets, certain experts (specifically Experts 1, 2, 5, and 6) consistently exhibit elevated activation rates. 4 Related Work 4.1 Dense and Sparse Models Traditional dense models activate all parameters during training and inference, leading to high computational and memory requirements as model sizes increase. In contrast, sparse models, employing the MoE architecture (Shazeer et al., 2017), activate only a subset of the total available parameters for each input token. In sparse models, the FFN layer is replaced by an MoE layer, directing each input token to a select group of expert networks for processing. The final token representation is 7 an amalgamation of outputs from these chosen experts. Despite an increase in parameters, the sparse activation of experts ensures computational efficiency while enh",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.02731#page=7,heuristic_unvalidated,29196
