4095909e92dd83b4,https://openalex.org/W4406755530,https://openalex.org/W4200634402,Parameters vs FLOPs: Scaling Laws for Optimal Sparsity for Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,GLaM: Efficient Scaling of Language Models with Mixture-of-Experts,10,Related Work,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"(2021) demonstrates GLaM’s superior efficiency and performance compared to GPT-3, showing that MoE architectures can achieve high performance with significantly lower computational and energy costs.",". A common theme in the literature suggests that training sparser models—achieved by increasing the number of smaller experts—offers significant gains in efficiency for both pretraining and inference phases. Through a comprehensive large-scale study, we provide empirical evidence for this, analyzing the impact of sparsity level on efficiency and defining optimal configurations. Supporting this, Du et al. (2021) demonstrates GLaM’s superior efficiency and performance compared to GPT-3, showing that MoE architectures can achieve high performance with significantly lower computational and energy costs. Further insights are offered by Clark et al. (2022), who analyze scaling behaviors across various MoE routing techniques. While their study finds that MoEs 10 generally outperform dense models, it also notes diminishing benefits as base model sizes g",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12370#page=10,heuristic_unvalidated,35035
1df2453f0e397f50,https://openalex.org/W4406755530,https://openalex.org/W4225390052,Parameters vs FLOPs: Scaling Laws for Optimal Sparsity for Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,ST-MoE: Designing Stable and Transferable Sparse Expert Models,2,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"We therefore ask “Can we draw scaling laws for the optimal trade-off between parameter count and FLOPs per example?” To address this question, we study sparse Mixture-of-Expert Transformers (MoEs) (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2022; Zoph et al., 2022; Muennighoff et al., 2024) in the context of language modeling.","ters and FLOPs per example are not directly linked, it is essential to jointly consider the effects of these variables on scaling model capacity (Clark et al., 2022). We therefore ask “Can we draw scaling laws for the optimal trade-off between parameter count and FLOPs per example?” To address this question, we study sparse Mixture-of-Expert Transformers (MoEs) (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2022; Zoph et al., 2022; Muennighoff et al., 2024) in the context of language modeling. Existing scaling law studies for MoEs, investigate the role of variables like number and granularity (Ludziejewski et al., 2024) of experts, underlying dense model size and inference compute in predicting the performance of the models under different conditions such as training or inference compute optimality (Du et al., 2021; Clark et al., 2022; Yun et al., 20",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2501.12370#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,4767
06f29f2c1799a1e5,https://openalex.org/W4407451021,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Uni-MoE: Scaling Unified Multimodal LLMs With Mixture of Experts,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,1,INTRODUCTION,numeric,unknown,"To alleviate this issue, there has been a shift towards integrating the Mixture of Experts (MoE) [17], [18] architecture • Yunxin Li, Shenyuan Jiang, Baotian Hu, Wanqi Zhong, and Min Zhang are with the Department of Computer Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen, China.","These developments underscore the increasing ability of MLLMs to process and reason across multiple modalities, showing the importance of both model scalability and the expansion of multimodal instructional data. However, scaling up model size usually incurs huge computational overhead in both the training and inference phases. To alleviate this issue, there has been a shift towards integrating the Mixture of Experts (MoE) [17], [18] architecture • Yunxin Li, Shenyuan Jiang, Baotian Hu, Wanqi Zhong, and Min Zhang are with the Department of Computer Science and Technology, Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen, China. (e-mail: liyunxin987@163.com, hubaotian@hit.edu.cn, and zhangmin2021@hit.edu.cn) • Wenhan Luo is an Associate Professor at the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Hong Kong. (e-mail: whluo.china@gmail.com) • Lin Ma is a R",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.11273#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,3352
