7fc6d19ab4316cff,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4391420732,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,LLaVA-MoLE: Sparse Mixture of LoRA Experts for Mitigating Data Conflicts in Instruction Finetuning MLLMs,13,Training,numeric,unknown,LLaVA-MoLE [113] extends the application of MoPE to multimodal tasks.,"ion. During the first stage, MixDA only fine-tunes the domain-adapters that work in parallel with the FFN to acquire domain-specific knowledge while simultaneously retaining the world knowledge. In the second stage, MixDA introduces a gating network and taskadapters on top of the FFN layer for tailoring the model to specific downstream tasks. This strategy allows for a more nuanced adaptation to the task at hand. LLaVA-MoLE [113] extends the application of MoPE to multimodal tasks. It creates a set of LoRA experts for the FFN layer to handle inputs from different domains, enhancing the model’s versatility. LLaVA-MoLE adopts a top-1 routing strategy, activating the most relevant expert based on the router’s output distribution, thus maintaining computational costs close to a standard FFN with LoRA. This framework is effective in addressing data con",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=13,heuristic_unvalidated,68111
8654b2f35893cad1,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4391420732,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,LLaVA-MoLE: Sparse Mixture of LoRA Experts for Mitigating Data Conflicts in Instruction Finetuning MLLMs,13,Training,name,unknown,"LLaVA-MoLE adopts a top-1 routing strategy, activating the most relevant expert based on the router’s output distribution, thus maintaining computational costs close to a standard FFN with LoRA.","ledge. In the second stage, MixDA introduces a gating network and taskadapters on top of the FFN layer for tailoring the model to specific downstream tasks. This strategy allows for a more nuanced adaptation to the task at hand. LLaVA-MoLE [113] extends the application of MoPE to multimodal tasks. It creates a set of LoRA experts for the FFN layer to handle inputs from different domains, enhancing the model’s versatility. LLaVA-MoLE adopts a top-1 routing strategy, activating the most relevant expert based on the router’s output distribution, thus maintaining computational costs close to a standard FFN with LoRA. This framework is effective in addressing data conflicts and consistently surpasses plainLoRA baselines across diverse data configurations. Contrasting with the MoPE implementations we have discussed, MixLoRA [112] creates a LoRA-MoE framewo",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=13,heuristic_unvalidated,68299
861b977d8faf736c,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4391420732,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,LLaVA-MoLE: Sparse Mixture of LoRA Experts for Mitigating Data Conflicts in Instruction Finetuning MLLMs,22,Training,numeric,unknown,"In parallel, to mitigate data conflicts, LLaVAMoLE [113] deploys a set of LoRA experts, specifically for the MLP layer, combined with a top-1 gating mechanism to refine instruction tuning in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs).","ontribute to the development of more efficient and effective multimodal learning systems. Furthermore, to address the specific issue of task conflicts in instruction tuning of Large VisionLanguage Models (LVLMs), MoCLE [44] integrates MoE with LoRA [172] experts and a distinct universal expert to activate task-specific model parameters based on clusters of instructions. In parallel, to mitigate data conflicts, LLaVAMoLE [113] deploys a set of LoRA experts, specifically for the MLP layer, combined with a top-1 gating mechanism to refine instruction tuning in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs). While the MLLMs employing MoE architectures have demonstrated impressive performances, they generally involve a limited number of experts and modalities [156]. To address this limitation, Li et al. [156] introduce the pioneering Uni-MoE, a unified MLL",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=22,heuristic_unvalidated,118233
defb39cc68a070c6,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4391420732,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,LLaVA-MoLE: Sparse Mixture of LoRA Experts for Mitigating Data Conflicts in Instruction Finetuning MLLMs,23,Training,numeric,unknown,"Recent studies [40], [43], [45], [46], [112], [113], [116] have demonstrated the efficacy of combining Parameter-efficient Finetuning (PEFT) techniques with MoE frameworks, offering a promising method for incorporating MoE into established LLMs.","l efficiency and the specialization of experts. Integration with Existing Frameworks. Ensuring seamless integration of MoE models into existing large language models (LLMs) is crucial for their broad adoption. It is particularly vital to enable adaptation of LLMs to MoE architecture without necessitating training from scratch, as it can significantly reduce resource consumption. Recent studies [40], [43], [45], [46], [112], [113], [116] have demonstrated the efficacy of combining Parameter-efficient Finetuning (PEFT) techniques with MoE frameworks, offering a promising method for incorporating MoE into established LLMs. However, these methods may compromise model performance or complicate the existing parallel strategies of pretraining and inference efforts [186]. Advancing the development of modular and plug-and-play MoE components is essential",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=23,heuristic_unvalidated,127835
