f2c037bb49efa9d6,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4394673388,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,"Dense Training, Sparse Inference: Rethinking Training of Mixture-of-Experts Language Models",16,Training,numeric,unknown,"8, AUGUST 2015 16 depicted in Figure 7, we divide the emerging schemes into three distinct categories: Dense-to-Sparse, which entails initiating with dense model training and progressively transitioning to a sparse MoE configuration [47], [60], [62], [65], [104], [120], [121], [122]; Sparse-to-Dense, which involves degrading a sparse MoE model to a dense form that is more conducive to hardware implementation for inference [50], [51], [125]; and Expert Models Merging, a process of integrating multiple pretrained dense expert models into a unified MoE model [52], [127], [128].","ce, enabling a flexible approach that synergizes the strengths of dense and sparse models while mitigating their respective weaknesses. As JOURNAL OF LATEX CLASS FILES, VOL. 14, NO. 8, AUGUST 2015 16 depicted in Figure 7, we divide the emerging schemes into three distinct categories: Dense-to-Sparse, which entails initiating with dense model training and progressively transitioning to a sparse MoE configuration [47], [60], [62], [65], [104], [120], [121], [122]; Sparse-to-Dense, which involves degrading a sparse MoE model to a dense form that is more conducive to hardware implementation for inference [50], [51], [125]; and Expert Models Merging, a process of integrating multiple pretrained dense expert models into a unified MoE model [52], [127], [128]. 4.4.1 Dense-to-Sparse To mitigate the training overhead associated with vision MoE transformer mo",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=16,heuristic_unvalidated,81065
21c54a00da753417,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4394673388,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,"Dense Training, Sparse Inference: Rethinking Training of Mixture-of-Experts Language Models",16,Training,numeric,unknown,"[62] posit that the parameter inefficiency observed in MoE models stems from conventional sparse training methodologies, where only a selected group of experts is engaged and refined for each input token.","ification of experts emerges during the process. Importantly, the Skywork-MoE analysis reveals that models with greater expert similarity tend to underperform, establishing expert similarity as a potential diagnostic tool during MoE training for upcycled models. Conversely, the expert similarity in models trained from scratch remains minimal, implying that non-uniform expert initialization promotes diversification. Pan et al. [62] posit that the parameter inefficiency observed in MoE models stems from conventional sparse training methodologies, where only a selected group of experts is engaged and refined for each input token. To counteract this, they introduce a hybrid framework for MoE models, denoted as DS-MoE, which integrates dense training (activating all the experts) with sparse inference (sparse expert activation) to achieve higher computatio",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=16,heuristic_unvalidated,86131
0c31e85c9a0876c9,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4394673388,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,"Dense Training, Sparse Inference: Rethinking Training of Mixture-of-Experts Language Models",23,Training,numeric,unknown,"A plethora of network architectures has been adopted as experts, including LSTM [24], CNN [97], [98], FFNs (MLPs) [25], [34], [35], [62], Attention [71], [80], and LoRA [43], [45], [112].","lopment, ensuring their gating decisions are transparent and trustworthy. Optimal Expert Architecture. The design of MoE architectures, encompassing the selection of network types and the quantity of experts, significantly influences the efficacy of multi-task learning across various domains. A plethora of network architectures has been adopted as experts, including LSTM [24], CNN [97], [98], FFNs (MLPs) [25], [34], [35], [62], Attention [71], [80], and LoRA [43], [45], [112]. Among these, FFNs as experts remain the most prevalent. Despite their considerable achievements, the exploration of various hybrids of network types within experts (as the distinct features processing capabilities of different network architectures), as well as the development of innovative expert architectures, remains nascent areas of research. Furthermore, the strategic",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=23,heuristic_unvalidated,126207
e8dd187b15a8b6e5,https://openalex.org/W4408858386,https://openalex.org/W4396815229,A Survey on Mixture of Experts in Large Language Models,"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model",1,INTRODUCTION,numeric,unknown,"As depicted in Figure 1, MoE has maintained a robust trajectory of growth, particularly notable in 2024 with the advent of Mixtral-8x7B [26] and a variety of subsequent industrial-scale LLMs such as Grok-1 [27], DBRX [28], Arctic [29], DeepSeek-V2 [30], etc.","flexible innovation has offered an effective approach for adhering to the scaling law, allowing for increased model capacity without a corresponding surge in computational demands. As depicted in Figure 1, MoE has maintained a robust trajectory of growth, particularly notable in 2024 with the advent of Mixtral-8x7B [26] and a variety of subsequent industrial-scale LLMs such as Grok-1 [27], DBRX [28], Arctic [29], DeepSeek-V2 [30], etc. Despite the growing popularity and application of mixture of experts (MoE) models across various domains, comprehensive reviews that thoroughly examine and categorize advancements, particularly in the context of MoE in LLMs, remain scarce. Specifically, we identified two surveys preceding our work: the first, published in August 2012 [31], provides a comprehensive review of early studies on dense MoE, which signif",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.06204#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,5395
