d69e57c8941d1760,https://openalex.org/W4411486231,https://openalex.org/W4402671950,Insights into DeepSeek-V3: Scaling Challenges and Reflections on Hardware for AI Architectures,DeepSeekMoE: Towards Ultimate Expert Specialization in Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,4,Model,name,unknown,"2.2 Cost-Effectiveness of MoE Models For sparse computing, we have developed DeepSeekMoE, an advanced Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture, which is illustrated in the lower right part of Figure 1.","st and model performance. In addition, approaches such as sparse attention [76], which seek to compress and sparsely activate attention keys and values, represent another attempt at overcoming the computational challenges associated with attention. We look forward to collaborative progress with the broader community toward breakthroughs in this area. 2.2 Cost-Effectiveness of MoE Models For sparse computing, we have developed DeepSeekMoE, an advanced Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture, which is illustrated in the lower right part of Figure 1. The advantages of MoE models lie in two folds. 2.2.1 Reducing Computational Requirements for Training. The primary advantage of the MoE architecture lies in its ability to significantly reduce training costs. By selectively activating only a subset of expert parameters, MoE models allow the total parameter c",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.09343#page=4,heuristic_unvalidated,18303
112f4792e384e42c,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W3040573126,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding,2,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"Recently, some works employ the MoE to scale the capacity of transformer-based models, especially the large language models (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Zoph et al., 2022).","s. The Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) structure is firstly applied in classical machine learning models by Jacobs et al. (1991) and Jordan and Jacobs (1994), then widely used in various deep learning models (Yuksel et al., 2012; Masoudnia and Ebrahimpour, 2014; Zhang et al., 2023). Recently, some works employ the MoE to scale the capacity of transformer-based models, especially the large language models (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Zoph et al., 2022). It adapts the original large feed-forward network (FFN) in each transformer block into multiple smaller FFNs, forming an expert layer with a router that computes the weighted output of each MoE layer. Sparse MoEs were first proposed by Fedus et al. (2022). In this approach, only a few experts are activated in each layer, accelerating training and inference while significantly increasing the number o",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,7047
52e6274eead07bf8,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,1,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"Notable examples of MoE models include Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), MixtralMoE (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b).","itecture is also proved effective. MoE widens the feed-forward network (FFN) layer (one expert) by having multiple parallel FFNs (experts). During forward propagation, only a subset of these experts is activated. Thus, compared to dense models, MoE models achieve better end-task performance and generalize better to new tasks without increasing computation costs. Notable examples of MoE models include Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), MixtralMoE (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b). *Work done during the internship at Microsoft Research. Despite significant progress in developing wider and deeper MoE LLMs, the increased memory consumption due to larger model sizes (i.e., increased number of experts) poses a substantial challenge to the deployment of these models in real-world settings. For example, storing and loading Mixtral8x7B, w",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,1924
7b1a973f15d74dc3,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Related Work,author_year,background_section,Sparse MoEs were first proposed by Fedus et al. (2022).,"Recently, some works employ the MoE to scale the capacity of transformer-based models, especially the large language models (Shazeer et al., 2017; Lepikhin et al., 2020; Zoph et al., 2022). It adapts the original large feed-forward network (FFN) in each transformer block into multiple smaller FFNs, forming an expert layer with a router that computes the weighted output of each MoE layer. Sparse MoEs were first proposed by Fedus et al. (2022). In this approach, only a few experts are activated in each layer, accelerating training and inference while significantly increasing the number of parameters for greater model capacity. Many sparse MoEs have been developed and opensourced within the AI community, such as Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), Mixtral-8B (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b). Recent studies also indicate that neural",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,7328
d43950cd3d3e43af,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"Many sparse MoEs have been developed and opensourced within the AI community, such as Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), Mixtral-8B (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b).","rt layer with a router that computes the weighted output of each MoE layer. Sparse MoEs were first proposed by Fedus et al. (2022). In this approach, only a few experts are activated in each layer, accelerating training and inference while significantly increasing the number of parameters for greater model capacity. Many sparse MoEs have been developed and opensourced within the AI community, such as Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), Mixtral-8B (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b). Recent studies also indicate that neural networks with the MoE structure exhibit better generalization ability compared to dense models (Zhu et al., 2022; Li et al., 2022). Model Pruning. Model pruning involves removing unimportant parameters from a well-trained neural network to balance task performance and computational efficiency (Liu et al., 2018; Wa",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,7647
84225ded4213582e,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W4407451021,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,Uni-MoE: Scaling Unified Multimodal LLMs With Mixture of Experts,1,Introduction,name,unknown,"Notable examples of MoE models include Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), MixtralMoE (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b).","ward network (FFN) layer (one expert) by having multiple parallel FFNs (experts). During forward propagation, only a subset of these experts is activated. Thus, compared to dense models, MoE models achieve better end-task performance and generalize better to new tasks without increasing computation costs. Notable examples of MoE models include Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), MixtralMoE (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b). *Work done during the internship at Microsoft Research. Despite significant progress in developing wider and deeper MoE LLMs, the increased memory consumption due to larger model sizes (i.e., increased number of experts) poses a substantial challenge to the deployment of these models in real-world settings. For example, storing and loading Mixtral8x7B, which has 8 experts in each of its 32 layers, requ",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,1984
b49f281ee0fdd3e2,https://openalex.org/W4412888953,https://openalex.org/W4407451021,Diversifying the Expert Knowledge for Task-Agnostic Pruning in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts,Uni-MoE: Scaling Unified Multimodal LLMs With Mixture of Experts,2,Related Work,name,background_section,"Many sparse MoEs have been developed and opensourced within the AI community, such as Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), Mixtral-8B (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b).","f each MoE layer. Sparse MoEs were first proposed by Fedus et al. (2022). In this approach, only a few experts are activated in each layer, accelerating training and inference while significantly increasing the number of parameters for greater model capacity. Many sparse MoEs have been developed and opensourced within the AI community, such as Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), Mixtral-8B (Jiang et al., 2024), and Uni-MoE (Li et al., 2024b). Recent studies also indicate that neural networks with the MoE structure exhibit better generalization ability compared to dense models (Zhu et al., 2022; Li et al., 2022). Model Pruning. Model pruning involves removing unimportant parameters from a well-trained neural network to balance task performance and computational efficiency (Liu et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2021). Pruning tec",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09590#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,7705
c4b953cf74614844,https://openalex.org/W4412945078,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Automatic Expert Discovery in LLM Upcycling via Sparse Interpolated Mixture-of-Experts,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,1,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"This tension has spurred growing interest in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures (Shazeer et al., 2017), which offer a promising pathway to flexible scaling of model capacity while maintaining inference efficiency by dynamically activating subsets of parameters per input for both training and inference.","ween scaling instruction-tuning data diversity, volume, and model capacity to achieve robust generalization (Wei et al., 2022; Longpre et al., 2023; Meta, *Corresponding authors; Equal contribution. 2024). However, balancing these dimensions efficiently without incurring prohibitive computational costs remains an open challenge. This tension has spurred growing interest in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures (Shazeer et al., 2017), which offer a promising pathway to flexible scaling of model capacity while maintaining inference efficiency by dynamically activating subsets of parameters per input for both training and inference. Yet, practical adoption of SMoEs in instruction fine-tuning is hindered by two key barriers: (i) the scarcity of publicly available pre-trained SMoE checkpoints (Muennighoff et al., 2025) and (ii) the immense computationa",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.12597#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2508
bb572ebf54cf757e,https://openalex.org/W4412945078,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Automatic Expert Discovery in LLM Upcycling via Sparse Interpolated Mixture-of-Experts,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,3,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"Early work has explored discrete expert-to-token routing – selecting only the most activated subsets of experts for each token in x (Shazeer et al., 2017; Riquelme et al., 2021; Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2021), while later work introduced alternatives, such as tokento-expert routing – choosing the top-scored subsets of tokens for each expert (Zhou et al., 2022), random and hash-based routing (Roller et al., 2021; Zuo et al., 2022).","tion of expert outputs: y = M X i=1 αi · fi(x), where α = h(x). (1) A defining feature of SMoE is its sparsity in expert activations: typically, only a subset of the most relevant experts is activated per input using the Top-K (Fedus et al., 2021) or Top-P (Huang et al., 2024) routing schemes. Early work has explored discrete expert-to-token routing – selecting only the most activated subsets of experts for each token in x (Shazeer et al., 2017; Riquelme et al., 2021; Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2021), while later work introduced alternatives, such as tokento-expert routing – choosing the top-scored subsets of tokens for each expert (Zhou et al., 2022), random and hash-based routing (Roller et al., 2021; Zuo et al., 2022). Advanced approaches further optimize routing stability and improve routing loadbalancing (Lewis et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2023).",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.12597#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,11325
