b234e286fb4a4b36,https://openalex.org/W4412945078,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Automatic Expert Discovery in LLM Upcycling via Sparse Interpolated Mixture-of-Experts,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,3,Related Work,author_year,background_section,"(1) A defining feature of SMoE is its sparsity in expert activations: typically, only a subset of the most relevant experts is activated per input using the Top-K (Fedus et al., 2021) or Top-P (Huang et al., 2024) routing schemes.","a set of M experts {f1, f2, . . . , fM}, and (2) a router function h : X →RM that outputs expert activations α = [α1, α2, . . . , αM] for input x. The SMoE output y is then given by a weighted combination of expert outputs: y = M X i=1 αi · fi(x), where α = h(x). (1) A defining feature of SMoE is its sparsity in expert activations: typically, only a subset of the most relevant experts is activated per input using the Top-K (Fedus et al., 2021) or Top-P (Huang et al., 2024) routing schemes. Early work has explored discrete expert-to-token routing – selecting only the most activated subsets of experts for each token in x (Shazeer et al., 2017; Riquelme et al., 2021; Lepikhin et al., 2021; Fedus et al., 2021), while later work introduced alternatives, such as tokento-expert routing – choosing the top-scored subsets of tokens for each expert (Zhou et al., 2022), ran",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.12597#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,11125
