354f9e8c269320a4,https://openalex.org/W4417073213,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Mixture of Group Experts for Learning Invariant Representations,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,1,Introduction,numeric,unknown,Sparsely activated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [26] offers a promising solution by utilizing a sparse architecture that activates only a subset of parameters during both training and inference.,"pts • Computing methodologies →Neural networks. Keywords Mixture-of-Experts, Group Sparsity, Invariant Representations 1 Introduction Transformer models [30] have consistently demonstrated performance improvements as their parameter counts increase [13]. However, the growth in model size comes with significant computational costs, making further scaling increasingly challenging. Sparsely activated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [26] offers a promising solution by utilizing a sparse architecture that activates only a subset of parameters during both training and inference. As shown in ∗Also with the Engineering Research Center of Intelligent Technology and Educational Application, Ministry of Education, Beijing 100816, China Gating Network top-2 softmax Router Input 0.4 0.1 0.3 0.1 0.1 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5 0 0 0 0.4 0.3 (a) Vanilla top-2 routing MoE layer gro",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09265#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2396
7567e23e0e24dace,https://openalex.org/W4417073213,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Mixture of Group Experts for Learning Invariant Representations,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown_negated_or_contrast,"While load balancing losses [19, 26] encourage even token distribution, they do not ensure that similar tokens activate similar experts.","outstanding performance in tasks such as image classification[9, 24] and language modeling [1, 28]. Despite their advantages, the vanilla MoE models suffer from insufficient diversity and specialization among experts [6, 27, 32]. In each MoE layer, the gating network determines token-to-expert assignments and should route similar tokens to the same experts to promote diversity and specialization. While load balancing losses [19, 26] encourage even token distribution, they do not ensure that similar tokens activate similar experts. As the number of experts grows, promoting diversity and specialization remains a challenge for MoE models. Contributions: In this paper, we reinterpret the combination of the most popular top-krouting and experts as a form of sparse representation. Building on this perspective, we propose a novel regularization technique",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09265#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,4729
bef0dc3ab3150525,https://openalex.org/W4417073213,https://openalex.org/W3040573126,Mixture of Group Experts for Learning Invariant Representations,GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown_negated_or_contrast,"While load balancing losses [19, 26] encourage even token distribution, they do not ensure that similar tokens activate similar experts.","outstanding performance in tasks such as image classification[9, 24] and language modeling [1, 28]. Despite their advantages, the vanilla MoE models suffer from insufficient diversity and specialization among experts [6, 27, 32]. In each MoE layer, the gating network determines token-to-expert assignments and should route similar tokens to the same experts to promote diversity and specialization. While load balancing losses [19, 26] encourage even token distribution, they do not ensure that similar tokens activate similar experts. As the number of experts grows, promoting diversity and specialization remains a challenge for MoE models. Contributions: In this paper, we reinterpret the combination of the most popular top-krouting and experts as a form of sparse representation. Building on this perspective, we propose a novel regularization technique",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09265#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,4729
c97420fc4d6abc56,https://openalex.org/W4417073213,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Mixture of Group Experts for Learning Invariant Representations,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Methodology,numeric,unknown,"Existing MoE models are typically derived from dense Transformer models by replacing certain feedforward layers with MoE layers [5, 12, 24].","among experts through an easily implementable approach, enabling the training of a greater number of experts and larger kvalues in top-krouting. 2 Methodology 2.1 Vanilla MoE To recap the core concepts behind our MoGE, we focus specifically on the sparsely activated MoE layer and the top-krouting. Existing MoE models are typically derived from dense Transformer models by replacing certain feedforward layers with MoE layers [5, 12, 24]. As illustrated in Fig. 1 (a), an MoE layer generally consists of a set of experts {E1(·), · · · , En(·)} and a router that directs input tokens to the most relevant experts. Here, ndenotes the total number of experts, and Ei(·) ∈Rmfor i= 1, · · · ,n. The router leverages the top-krouting mechanism to reduce computation overhead by selectively activating only the top-kexperts deemed most relevant to each input token. It typ",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.09265#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,7700
