a27e849672417242,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,1,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"1, skipping 83% of the experts in previous methods [6, 22, 42] during inference results in accuracy drops of over 10%.","28, 42] due to a fixed number of activated experts for all tokens, which can incur significant inference inefficiency [28, 42, 66]. Recent expert skipping methods [6, 22, 42] thus propose to skip redundant experts w.r.t. current tokens to accelerate inference. However, applying these methods to MoE MLLMs leads to a significant drop in accuracy. For example, as shown in Fig. 1, skipping 83% of the experts in previous methods [6, 22, 42] during inference results in accuracy drops of over 10%. To solve the problem, we first make in-depth analyses and obtain two key insights overlooked before: (i) The contributions of experts to the model outputs vary significantly arXiv:2511.15690v2 [cs.CV] 22 Feb 2026 across layers. Specifically, experts in shallow layers play far more critical roles than those in deeper layers. However, prior works [6, 22, 42] only cons",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,4000
ade66ae3010c56cc,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"However, prior works [6, 22, 42] only consider intra-layer information (e.g., Eq.","s methods [6, 22, 42] during inference results in accuracy drops of over 10%. To solve the problem, we first make in-depth analyses and obtain two key insights overlooked before: (i) The contributions of experts to the model outputs vary significantly arXiv:2511.15690v2 [cs.CV] 22 Feb 2026 across layers. Specifically, experts in shallow layers play far more critical roles than those in deeper layers. However, prior works [6, 22, 42] only consider intra-layer information (e.g., Eq. (1)) to develop skipping schedules. (ii) Tokens of different modalities (i.e., text and vision) exhibit distinct behaviors as they pass through experts, and experts have a larger effect on updating text tokens. Yet prior works mainly study unimodal LLMs [29] and do not account for this modality gap in MLLMs. These observations underscore the need for a modality-specific",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,4420
099687489c253408,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,2,Related Work,numeric,background_section,"Owing to the modular and sparse nature of MoE, a new line of research—expert skipping—has emerged, which dynamically bypasses redundant experts [6, 22, 42] to speed up inference.","tly retraining and extensive data access. In contrast, training-free techniques [18, 43, 61] enable lightweight efficiency enhancement without modifying the training pipeline, including quantization for parameter compression [14, 29] and pruning for structural sparsity [30, 63]. Owing to the modular and sparse nature of MoE, a new line of research—expert skipping—has emerged, which dynamically bypasses redundant experts [6, 22, 42] to speed up inference. Among these studies, Lu et al. [42] utilize dynamic expert skipping based on expert routing probabilities. MC-MoE [22] further integrates an attention-aware expert protection approach during skipping and combines mixed-precision quantization for expert compression. Additionally, DiEP [6] introduces a differentiable expert pruning framework with adaptive expert skipping, which jointly considers rou",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,8105
0366c29a8099c73d,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,3,Related Work,numeric,background_section,"Motivation Existing studies [6, 22, 42] have found that not every selected expert provides essential contributions for tokens.","o ensure sparse activation, only a subset of experts is executed. Let S(l) denote the indices of the top-k experts with the largest routing probabilities. The output y(l) of the MoE layer is obtained through a weighted aggregation: y(l+1) = X m∈S(l) π(l) m · Expert(l) m (x(l)). (2) This formulation allows the model to scale the number of parameters independently of the active computation cost. 4. Motivation Existing studies [6, 22, 42] have found that not every selected expert provides essential contributions for tokens. They thus propose to skip the computation of unimportant experts to improve inference efficiency. However, they focus on text-only LLMs [27]. In this study, we have identified that directly adapting these methods to MoE MLLMs [26, 50] overlooks two key factors: Global contribution (Sec. 4.1) and modality gap (Sec. 4.2). Both factors s",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,10596
fd2c329cf2b433ec,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,3,Related Work,numeric,background_section,"Global Contribution Disregard Recent skipping strategies [6, 22, 42] rely on the local routing probabilities (Eq.","- tant experts to improve inference efficiency. However, they focus on text-only LLMs [27]. In this study, we have identified that directly adapting these methods to MoE MLLMs [26, 50] overlooks two key factors: Global contribution (Sec. 4.1) and modality gap (Sec. 4.2). Both factors significantly affect the performance and efficiency of expert skipping in MLLMs. 4.1. Global Contribution Disregard Recent skipping strategies [6, 22, 42] rely on the local routing probabilities (Eq. (1)) to determine the skipping schedule of the l-th layer, reflecting only input-dependent gating within a single layer. Such layer-agnostic rules ignore the global contribution (i.e., impact on final outputs) imbalance of experts across different layers. Empirically, as shown in Fig. 2, we observe that when reducing the value of k for expert routing, shallower layers incur",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,11179
6ca4558ff6cdd97e,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,3,Related Work,numeric,background_section,"Accordingly, the aforementioned layer-independent expert skipping strategies [6, 22, 42] risk excessive skipping at shallow layers, which are critical to final outputs, and vice versa for deep layers.","hown in Fig. 2, we observe that when reducing the value of k for expert routing, shallower layers incur much severe performance drops than those of deeper layers. This may result from that, relative to the error of deeper layers, errors introduced in shallow layers are amplified by subsequent layers [23], leading to a significant error explosion. Accordingly, the aforementioned layer-independent expert skipping strategies [6, 22, 42] risk excessive skipping at shallow layers, which are critical to final outputs, and vice versa for deep layers. Insight (i): The observation yields a core design principle: With higher global contributions, experts in shallowcritical layers should be preserved; while experts in deeper, less influential ones can be skipped more aggressively. 4.2. Modality Gap Matters Focusing on expert skipping for MLLMs, we further exam-",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,11947
9fe394d24091399a,https://openalex.org/W7106330638,https://openalex.org/W4402671637,MoDES: Accelerating Mixture-of-Experts Multimodal Large Language Models via Dynamic Expert Skipping,Not All Experts are Equal: Efficient Expert Pruning and Skipping for Mixture-of-Experts Large Language Models,5,evaluation,numeric,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"As there is no expert skipping baselines for MLLMs and previous methods for LLMs only consider models with top-2 routing in practice, we re-implement and adjust them to top-k (k > 2) settings for MLLMs: For the l-th layer, NAEE [42] originally skips the top-2 expert if π(l) top-2 < β(l) · π(l) top-1, where π(l) top-1 and π(l) top-2 denotes the top-1 and top-2 routing probabilities (Eq.","Benchval,v [59] (LVB), and VideoMMMU [20] (VMMMU). lmms-eval [67] is utilized to perform the above evaluation. For MMBench and MMVet, we use DeepSeek-V3.1 [12] to rate the generated texts. Baselines. As there is no expert skipping baselines for MLLMs and previous methods for LLMs only consider models with top-2 routing in practice, we re-implement and adjust them to top-k (k > 2) settings for MLLMs: For the l-th layer, NAEE [42] originally skips the top-2 expert if π(l) top-2 < β(l) · π(l) top-1, where π(l) top-1 and π(l) top-2 denotes the top-1 and top-2 routing probabilities (Eq. (1)). β(l) is a hyperparameter. Here, we adapt this strategy, referring to the Appendix of NAEE, to a more general top-k scenario. Specifically, top-i to top-k experts are skipped if Pk u=i π(l) top-u < β(l) · Pk v=1 π(l) top-v. We also apply similar adjustments for MC",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.15690#page=5,heuristic_unvalidated,22934
