27be3e106f2fa56f,https://openalex.org/W7106673403,https://openalex.org/W4402671950,OrdMoE: Preference Alignment via Hierarchical Expert Group Ranking in Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts LLMs,DeepSeekMoE: Towards Ultimate Expert Specialization in Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"In parallel, the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture has emerged as the de facto standard for scaling modern MLLMs [1, 7, 10, 22].","may not reflect genuine multimodal quality and can introduce bias when the perturbation does not align with semantic meaning. In both cases, the preference signal is imposed from outside the model’s natural computation, rather arXiv:2511.19023v1 [cs.LG] 24 Nov 2025 than emerging from its internal dynamics. In parallel, the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture has emerged as the de facto standard for scaling modern MLLMs [1, 7, 10, 22]. By sparsely activating specialized subnetworks (experts) conditioned on input tokens, MoE enables substantial gains in both model capacity and computational efficiency. Critically, we observe that this routing mechanism implicitly encodes a rich, fine-grained preference signal. Specifically, the router’s expert selection logits reflect an internal assessment of which experts are most likely to generate high-quality out",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.19023#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,3975
7ee88a0aec7cb926,https://openalex.org/W7106673403,https://openalex.org/W4402671950,OrdMoE: Preference Alignment via Hierarchical Expert Group Ranking in Multimodal Mixture-of-Experts LLMs,DeepSeekMoE: Towards Ultimate Expert Specialization in Mixture-of-Experts Language Models,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"Subsequently, DeepSeek-MoE [7] further advanced the MoE framework by introducing the concepts of fine-grained experts and shared experts.","-based multimodal large language models. 2. Related Works 2.1. MoE-based MLLMs The Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) [9] is a neural network architecture designed to scale model capacity through sparse activation and conditional computation. Mixtral 8x7B [10], a prominent sparse MoE model based on the Mistral architecture, notably uses a router to select two out of eight experts per token at every layer. Subsequently, DeepSeek-MoE [7] further advanced the MoE framework by introducing the concepts of fine-grained experts and shared experts. The successful application of MoE in LLMs showcases the architecture’s efficiency to achieve strong performance at a significantly lower computational expense. Recently, some works [1, 12, 15, 22] have integrated the MoE into MLLMs. For instance, Kimi-VL [22] employs the Moonlight MoE language decoder, which has a to",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.19023#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6862
742d4dee860d94ed,https://openalex.org/W7106698664,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,AnyExperts: On-Demand Expert Allocation for Multimodal Language Models with Mixture of Expert,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"Among these, topK routing [14, 31, 37] is the most widely adopted approach.","GShard [16] pioneeringly applied MoE to translation tasks for large-scale language models, the MoE architecture has demonstrated strong capabilities. The milestone Switch Transformers [10], by extending the MoE method, achieved unprecedented model scale and training efficiency in visual understanding tasks. Most routing mechanisms in MoE models assign a fixed number of experts to each token. Among these, topK routing [14, 31, 37] is the most widely adopted approach. It computes a gating score reflecting each expert’s suitability for the input token, then selects the topK experts with the highest scores for activation. Subsequent work [10, 16, 35, 43] has primarily focused on balancing token assignments across experts under the constraint of fixed top-K routing. Other efforts have extended MoE models to multimodal domains: for instance, Uni",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18314#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,9548
00f7e85fada3ea0d,https://openalex.org/W7106698664,https://openalex.org/W3040573126,AnyExperts: On-Demand Expert Allocation for Multimodal Language Models with Mixture of Expert,GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"Static Routing MoE Architectures Since models such as GShard [16] pioneeringly applied MoE to translation tasks for large-scale language models, the MoE architecture has demonstrated strong capabilities.","or multimodal Mixture-of-Experts models, which allocates a variable number of experts per token based on its semantic importance. • AnyExperts consistently boosts performance under the same expert budget, and reduces compute at parity, across diverse tasks, demonstrating that importance-driven allocation maximizes the utility of every expert. 2. Related Works 2.1. Static Routing MoE Architectures Since models such as GShard [16] pioneeringly applied MoE to translation tasks for large-scale language models, the MoE architecture has demonstrated strong capabilities. The milestone Switch Transformers [10], by extending the MoE method, achieved unprecedented model scale and training efficiency in visual understanding tasks. Most routing mechanisms in MoE models assign a fixed number of experts to each token. Among these, topK routing [14, 31, 37]",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18314#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,9126
1409409039130b24,https://openalex.org/W7106698664,https://openalex.org/W3040573126,AnyExperts: On-Demand Expert Allocation for Multimodal Language Models with Mixture of Expert,GShard: Scaling Giant Models with Conditional Computation and Automatic Sharding,2,Introduction,numeric,unknown,"Subsequent work [10, 16, 35, 43] has primarily focused on balancing token assignments across experts under the constraint of fixed top-K routing.","ieved unprecedented model scale and training efficiency in visual understanding tasks. Most routing mechanisms in MoE models assign a fixed number of experts to each token. Among these, topK routing [14, 31, 37] is the most widely adopted approach. It computes a gating score reflecting each expert’s suitability for the input token, then selects the topK experts with the highest scores for activation. Subsequent work [10, 16, 35, 43] has primarily focused on balancing token assignments across experts under the constraint of fixed top-K routing. Other efforts have extended MoE models to multimodal domains: for instance, UniMoE [22] supports speech, image-text, and video inputs, while DeepSeek-MoE [8] and XMoE [38] introduce finegrained expert segmentation into the MoE architecture. Despite these advances, all aforementioned approaches adhere to t",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.18314#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,9777
