dbe98ae55710676e,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,7,Experiments,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"We compare three MoE methods including Switch Transformer top-1 gating (ST Top-1), GShard top-2 gating (GS Top-2) and our method (EC-CF2) that matches the activation memory size and computational cost of GS Top-2.","methods work well in terms of perplexity on the evaluation dataset during pre-training. As demonstrated in Fig. 2 (b), having more experts consistently improves training perplexity. 4.4 Fine-tuning on GLUE and SuperGLUE To validate whether improved perplexity directly translates to better performance in downstream tasks, we perform fine-tuning on 11 selected tasks from GLUE and SuperGLUE. We compare three MoE methods including Switch Transformer top-1 gating (ST Top-1), GShard top-2 gating (GS Top-2) and our method (EC-CF2) that matches the activation memory size and computational cost of GS Top-2. Indicated by the results in Table 2, our EC-CF2 method consistently outperforms the related methods and yields more than 2% average accuracy increase in a large 8B/64E setting. Table 3 further compares our 8B/64E model against its dense counterpart. Again, our method achie",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09368#page=7,heuristic_unvalidated,24440
dca48aec85a08fd6,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,8,Method,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,4.7 Ablation Capacity Factor: We study the capacity factor in our expert choice method and compare the training perplexity with the baseline top-1 gating method used in Switch Transformer.,"s specialized experts. The fine-tuning results are presented in the last row in Table 4. Hashing based routing performs worse than expert choice in terms of average scores and variance. This indicates that load balancing alone does not generate all the benefits. 4.7 Ablation Capacity Factor: We study the capacity factor in our expert choice method and compare the training perplexity with the baseline top-1 gating method used in Switch Transformer. As described in Eq. (1), the capacity factor determines how many experts on average each token can be routed to, thus the bucket size k of each expert. In all our previous experiments, we use a capacity factor of 2, which matches the computational footprint of the top-2 gating used in GShard method. To match the computation cost on a per-token basis fairly with top-1 gating used in Switch Transformer, we reduce the capacity",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09368#page=8,heuristic_unvalidated,27820
635d67093e5c58bd,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,8,Method,name,unknown,"To match the computation cost on a per-token basis fairly with top-1 gating used in Switch Transformer, we reduce the capacity factor to 1 and plot the training perplexity in Fig.","-1 gating method used in Switch Transformer. As described in Eq. (1), the capacity factor determines how many experts on average each token can be routed to, thus the bucket size k of each expert. In all our previous experiments, we use a capacity factor of 2, which matches the computational footprint of the top-2 gating used in GShard method. To match the computation cost on a per-token basis fairly with top-1 gating used in Switch Transformer, we reduce the capacity factor to 1 and plot the training perplexity in Fig. 4 (a). Not surprisingly, using a smaller capacity factor yields higher perplexity, but our method still significantly outperforms top-1 gating. We further push the capacity factor down to 0.5, and observe that it still outperforms the top-1 gating. Comparison with Dense Models on Pre-training: We compare our method with dense models on pre-training. A",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09368#page=8,heuristic_unvalidated,28225
425011c9aee27b51,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,https://openalex.org/W4200634402,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,GLaM: Efficient Scaling of Language Models with Mixture-of-Experts,6,Experiments,name,use_signal,Dataset: We use the high-quality dataset from GLaM [?,"one expert. While nparams is the total number of trainable parameters, nact−params represents the number of activated parameters per token. L is the total number of Transformer layers, M is the model dimension, H is the hidden dimension after the projection in each transformer layer, nheads is the number of attention heads, and dhead is the hidden dimension of each attention head. Dataset: We use the high-quality dataset from GLaM [? ] of 1.6 trillion tokens that are representative of a wide range of natural language use cases. An in-house classifier is trained to classify between a collection of curated text and other webpages and estimate the content quality of a webpage. A high-quality filtered subset of webpages are combined with books, Wikipedia pages, conversations, forums, and news to create the final dataset. The data and mixture weights can be fo",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09368#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,19738
eefdc45ac80e7f29,https://openalex.org/W7133198209,https://openalex.org/W4200634402,Mixture-Of-Experts with Expert Choice Routing,GLaM: Efficient Scaling of Language Models with Mixture-of-Experts,6,Experiments,name,unknown,The data and mixture weights can be found in Table 3 in the GLaM paper.,"tokens that are representative of a wide range of natural language use cases. An in-house classifier is trained to classify between a collection of curated text and other webpages and estimate the content quality of a webpage. A high-quality filtered subset of webpages are combined with books, Wikipedia pages, conversations, forums, and news to create the final dataset. The data and mixture weights can be found in Table 3 in the GLaM paper. Model Training: Our model training follows the setups of GLaM [? ] where a maximum sequence length of 1024 tokens is adopted. We use an Adafactor optimizer [32] with first-moment decay β1 = 0 and second-moment decay β2 = 0.99. We keep the learning rate constant for the first 10K training steps, and then decay it with an inverse square root schedule. Unlike most related works, we do not impose any auxiliary loss for load",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.09368#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,20194
f0926620fbdb1e8c,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,1,Introduction,author_year,unknown,"Mixture-of-experts (MoE) models (Shazeer et al., 2017) have emerged as a promising architecture for their ability to scale model capacity (in terms of number of parameters) without incurring proportional overhead at inference.","020; Hoffmann et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2025), where scaling up model and data size is crucial to improve model performance. However, classical large models often face trade-offs between computational efficiency and representational power, especially when deployed in real-world scenarios where resource constraints and robustness are critical considerations (He et al., 2024; Dai et al., 2024). Mixture-of-experts (MoE) models (Shazeer et al., 2017) have emerged as a promising architecture for their ability to scale model capacity (in terms of number of parameters) without incurring proportional overhead at inference. Unlike traditional dense models, which activate all parameters for every input, MoE models dynamically route input data to a subset of specialized experts tailored to process particular characteristics of the data. This routing mechanism enables MoE models",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,2720
eb2c8df08e633bc6,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W2581624817,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Outrageously Large Neural Networks: The Sparsely-Gated Mixture-of-Experts Layer,2,Related work,author_year,background_section,"Modern advances have significantly scaled MoE architectures to efficiently accommodate extremely large model sizes, exemplified by sparsely gated MoE layers proposed by Shazeer et al (Shazeer et al., 2017).","oE models were first introduced by Jacobs et al. (Jacobs et al., 1991) as a dynamic mechanism to combine multiple specialized sub-models through gating functions, with subsequent hierarchical extensions presented by Jordan and Jacobs (Jordan and Jacobs, 1994). Modern advances have significantly scaled MoE architectures to efficiently accommodate extremely large model sizes, exemplified by sparsely gated MoE layers proposed by Shazeer et al (Shazeer et al., 2017). Further efficiency improvements were realized with the Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), simplifying the routing mechanism by activating only one expert per input token. Additional strategies, such as BASE layers (Lewis et al., 2021) and hash-based routing techniques (Roller et al., 2021), have been explored to mitigate issues related to load imbalance and training inefficiency. MoE architectures have demonstrated su",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6663
9a93441e89c00752,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,1,unresolved,name,unknown,"Traditional MoE methods, such as the Switch Transformer, route experts independently within each layer.","Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition Zijin Gu∗, Tatiana Likhomanenko, Navdeep Jaitly Apple Mixture-of-experts (MoE) architectures have expanded from language modeling to automatic speech recognition (ASR). Traditional MoE methods, such as the Switch Transformer, route experts independently within each layer. Our analysis reveals that routers in most layers make expert choices that are not strongly correlated with the choices of the routers in other layers. To increase the cooperation between experts in different layers and encourage greater specialization, we use a shared router across different MoE layers. We call this model Omni-router Transformer. Extensive experiments on a large",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=1,heuristic_unvalidated,297
8b802d8ae7253c28,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Introduction,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"Empirical results across multiple model configurations consistently indicate that Omni-router delivers superior lower training error, test word error rates (WER) and robustness against highly diverse data compared to traditional dense models and Switch Transformer baselines across diverse out-of-domain evaluation datasets.","residual connections and only change minimally from layer to layer (Sun et al., 2025), and thus sharing a decision boundaries across layers is possible by just using a shared router. Empirical results across multiple model configurations consistently indicate that Omni-router delivers superior lower training error, test word error rates (WER) and robustness against highly diverse data compared to traditional dense models and Switch Transformer baselines across diverse out-of-domain evaluation datasets. 2 Related work MoE models were first introduced by Jacobs et al. (Jacobs et al., 1991) as a dynamic mechanism to combine multiple specialized sub-models through gating functions, with subsequent hierarchical extensions presented by Jordan and Jacobs (Jordan and Jacobs, 1994). Modern advances have significantly scaled MoE architectures to efficiently accommodate extre",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6138
52aa4a3aedad5776,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,2,Related work,author_year,background_section,"Further efficiency improvements were realized with the Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), simplifying the routing mechanism by activating only one expert per input token.","specialized sub-models through gating functions, with subsequent hierarchical extensions presented by Jordan and Jacobs (Jordan and Jacobs, 1994). Modern advances have significantly scaled MoE architectures to efficiently accommodate extremely large model sizes, exemplified by sparsely gated MoE layers proposed by Shazeer et al (Shazeer et al., 2017). Further efficiency improvements were realized with the Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022), simplifying the routing mechanism by activating only one expert per input token. Additional strategies, such as BASE layers (Lewis et al., 2021) and hash-based routing techniques (Roller et al., 2021), have been explored to mitigate issues related to load imbalance and training inefficiency. MoE architectures have demonstrated substantial success across various domains, including natural language 2 Figure 2 Visualization o",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=2,heuristic_unvalidated,6776
1c155e34dfca2b4e,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,3,Related work,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"First, we introduce an innovative shared-routing approach within Transformer-based MoE ASR models, demonstrating its effectiveness in terms of both accuracy and robustness compared to conventional dense and Switch Transformer MoE architectures.","training robustness (Zoph et al., 2022). Such issues motivate the exploration of novel routing mechanisms aimed at enhancing MoE model robustness and effectiveness. Our contributions address these gaps in two primary ways. First, we introduce an innovative shared-routing approach within Transformer-based MoE ASR models, demonstrating its effectiveness in terms of both accuracy and robustness compared to conventional dense and Switch Transformer MoE architectures. Second, our proposed MoE design remains straightforward, eliminating the need for auxiliary embedding networks, complex auxiliary loss combinations (You et al., 2021, 2022), or explicit inductive biases such as accent or language embeddings (You et al., 2022; Kwon and Chung, 2023), thus facilitating simpler implementation and training while being effective. 3 3 Background 3.1 Mixture-of-Experts layer Most",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=3,heuristic_unvalidated,9638
329812bab5a20457,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,4,Background,author_year,unknown_negated_or_contrast,"3.2 Switch Transformer Instead of choosing top-k experts based on the router’s decision, Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022) simplifies the routing mechanism and chooses only the top-1 expert.","uter in a layer l, and softmax is taken along the second dimension. The top-k experts of a specific token Xl i ∈RD are then selected for routing it to give output Y l i ∈RT ×D′: Y l i = X j∈K [P l(Xl)]j i [Ej(Xl)]i, (3.2) where K is a set of indices of top-k experts, and Ej(·) ∈RT ×D′ represents the output of expert j. 3.2 Switch Transformer Instead of choosing top-k experts based on the router’s decision, Switch Transformer (Fedus et al., 2022) simplifies the routing mechanism and chooses only the top-1 expert. It was shown that switch layer not only reduces the router computation cost but also gives better performance in language models. Throughout this paper, we also use only the top-1 expert. 3.3 Load balancing loss During training of MoE models, an auxiliary loss is often introduced to maintain the balance of load (the number of tokens that go through one exper",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=4,heuristic_unvalidated,10978
b64186cde9fc830a,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,4,Background,author_year,background_section,"As introduced in (Fedus et al., 2022), given N experts and T tokens Xl for layer l, the fraction of tokens dispatched to each expert j, f l, and the fraction of router probability allocated for each expert j, ρl, are defined as f l j = 1 T T X i=1 1{arg max j′ [P l(Xl)]j′ i = j}, (3.3) ρl j = 1 T X i:arg maxj′[P l(Xl)]j′ i =j [P l(Xl)]j i, (3.4) the load balancing loss is defined as","d chooses only the top-1 expert. It was shown that switch layer not only reduces the router computation cost but also gives better performance in language models. Throughout this paper, we also use only the top-1 expert. 3.3 Load balancing loss During training of MoE models, an auxiliary loss is often introduced to maintain the balance of load (the number of tokens that go through one expert) of each expert. As introduced in (Fedus et al., 2022), given N experts and T tokens Xl for layer l, the fraction of tokens dispatched to each expert j, f l, and the fraction of router probability allocated for each expert j, ρl, are defined as f l j = 1 T T X i=1 1{arg max j′ [P l(Xl)]j′ i = j}, (3.3) ρl j = 1 T X i:arg maxj′[P l(Xl)]j′ i =j [P l(Xl)]j i, (3.4) the load balancing loss is defined as Lload = PL l=1 Ll load, where each Ll load is a scaled dot product: Ll load = N",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=4,heuristic_unvalidated,11463
598b931795f249d2,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,4,Background,name,background_section,"(3.5) 4 Omni-router: sharing routing decisions across MoE layers In conventional MoE architectures, such as the Switch Transformer, each MoE layer independently selects its experts without explicit coordination among layers.","obability allocated for each expert j, ρl, are defined as f l j = 1 T T X i=1 1{arg max j′ [P l(Xl)]j′ i = j}, (3.3) ρl j = 1 T X i:arg maxj′[P l(Xl)]j′ i =j [P l(Xl)]j i, (3.4) the load balancing loss is defined as Lload = PL l=1 Ll load, where each Ll load is a scaled dot product: Ll load = N N X j=1 f l j · ρl j. (3.5) 4 Omni-router: sharing routing decisions across MoE layers In conventional MoE architectures, such as the Switch Transformer, each MoE layer independently selects its experts without explicit coordination among layers. Although SGD facilitates implicit coordination between these layers during training, the inherent randomness introduced by SGD can lead the model to function more as an ensemble of loosely coupled experts rather than as a tightly integrated system. This phenomenon is analogous to the effect observed in dropout training, where the mod",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=4,heuristic_unvalidated,12045
f7a623beb4f84fd0,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,5,Background,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,5.2 ASR models We compare our proposed Omni-router Transformer model against two baseline architectures: a dense transformer and a Switch Transformer.,"Fleurs (Conneau et al., 2023), Librispeech (Panayotov et al., 2015), Switchboard (Godfrey et al., 1992), Tedlium (Hernandez et al., 2018), Voxpopuli (Wang et al., 2021), and Wall Street Journal (Paul and Baker, 1992). Importantly, none of the selected test datasets overlap with the training data. 5.2 ASR models We compare our proposed Omni-router Transformer model against two baseline architectures: a dense transformer and a Switch Transformer. All ASR models utilize the standard Transformer encoder architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017), optimized using CTC (Graves and Graves, 2012) and employing an 8k-token word-piece vocabulary derived from the training transcriptions. Baseline 1: Dense Transformer We train three dense transformer models of varying sizes—84M, 140M, and 200M parameters—with corresponding embedding dimensions of 512, 768, and 1024, respectively. Al",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=5,heuristic_unvalidated,15877
80546f9ef06642a5,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,5,Background,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"Baseline 2: Switch Transformer Corresponding to each dense model, we train a Switch Transformer model by replacing the FFN layers with MoE layers.","17), optimized using CTC (Graves and Graves, 2012) and employing an 8k-token word-piece vocabulary derived from the training transcriptions. Baseline 1: Dense Transformer We train three dense transformer models of varying sizes—84M, 140M, and 200M parameters—with corresponding embedding dimensions of 512, 768, and 1024, respectively. All dense models consist of 16 encoder blocks, each with a FFN dimension of 4096. Baseline 2: Switch Transformer Corresponding to each dense model, we train a Switch Transformer model by replacing the FFN layers with MoE layers. Each expert within the MoE layers retains the same FFN structure as the corresponding dense baseline. During both training and inference, each layer dynamically selects the top-1 expert based on the router’s decision. Proposed Model: Omni-router Transformer For each dense model configuration, we train an Omnir",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=5,heuristic_unvalidated,16417
dc8e8ee3e8fa86c1,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,6,Background,name,comparison_or_baseline_signal,"Apart from this shared-routing mechanism, the Omni-router Transformer maintains the same configuration as the Switch Transformer baseline.","aining and inference, each layer dynamically selects the top-1 expert based on the router’s decision. Proposed Model: Omni-router Transformer For each dense model configuration, we train an Omnirouter Transformer model, introducing a shared router across all layers to encourage consistent token routing 5 decisions. Apart from this shared-routing mechanism, the Omni-router Transformer maintains the same configuration as the Switch Transformer baseline. 5.3 Training setup All ASR models take as input 80-dimensional log-mel filterbank features computed using a 25ms sliding window with a 10ms stride. Subsequently, every four consecutive frames are stacked before being fed into the transformer layers. We apply SpecAugment (Park et al., 2019) as data augmentation, employing 2 frequency masks (maximum width 30) and 10 time masks (maximum width 50, ratio 0.1). Training b",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,17098
3cfb4c294151dcc8,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,6,Background,name,background_section,"However, for some Switch Transformer models, we reduced the peak LR and adjusted the load balancing loss weight to address observed training instability.","lateaus. Hyperparameters were first optimized using the baseline dense ASR model (84M parameters) and subsequently held constant for all dense and MoE variants. This approach was motivated by the higher computational cost of tuning MoE models. By tuning the dense model and scaling to MoE, we aimed to validate the generalization capabilities of MoE architectures while enabling resource-efficient optimization. However, for some Switch Transformer models, we reduced the peak LR and adjusted the load balancing loss weight to address observed training instability. 5.4 Evaluation We evaluate the final model performance by computing word error rates (WER) using greedy decoding. Before calculating WER, we apply the Whisper normalizer described in (Radford et al., 2023) to both ground truth and predicted transcriptions. 6 Results 6.1 Omni-router improves ASR In Table 1, we p",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,18652
3bfb971262b48dbc,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,6,Evaluation,name,unknown,"Additionally, the Omni-router Transformer further improves upon the performance of the Switch Transformer.","i-router improves ASR In Table 1, we present the WER results comparing a baseline dense ASR model (140M) with Switch Transformer and Omni-router Transformer ASR models, each employing two experts per layer. We first note that both MoE-based architectures consistently outperform the dense baseline across all evaluated out-of-domain datasets. Additionally, the Omni-router Transformer further improves upon the performance of the Switch Transformer. Given that the Omni-router shares hyperparameters originally optimized for the Switch Transformer, its superior performance despite minimal hyperparameter tuning underscores the effectiveness and robustness of the shared-routing strategy. 6.2 Why Does the Omni-router Transformer Outperform A central question raised by our experimental results is what factors contribute to the superior performance of the Omni-router Transform",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,19492
76eddaf558b0279d,https://openalex.org/W7148398150,https://openalex.org/W3119866685,Omni-Router: Sharing Routing Decisions in Sparse Mixture-of-Experts for Speech Recognition,Switch Transformers: Scaling to Trillion Parameter Models with Simple and Efficient Sparsity,6,Evaluation,name,unknown,"Each sample visualization consists of (from top to bottom): the mel-spectrogram, the expert assignments for a 4-expert Switch Transformer, and the expert assignments for a 4-expert Omni-router Transformer.","etwork, enabling the model to develop more cooperative and specialized experts. To investigate this hypothesis, we conducted a detailed analysis of expert utilization, as illustrated in Fig. 2. We visualize expert assignments across three randomly selected audio samples from the Librispeech dev-other dataset. Each sample visualization consists of (from top to bottom): the mel-spectrogram, the expert assignments for a 4-expert Switch Transformer, and the expert assignments for a 4-expert Omni-router Transformer. Expert assignments are plotted with time steps on the horizontal axis and transformer layers on the vertical axis. The colors representing experts have no inherent meaning, and for clarity in Switch Transformer plots, colors are permuted to optimally align across layers. 6 Table 1 WERs of a dense ASR, a 2-expert Switch Transformer ASR and a 2-expert Omni-rou",https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.05724#page=6,heuristic_unvalidated,20488
